    "status": "approved",
    "summary": "What a memory is, as types, before anything can store one. A memory is a claim by a named actor, from a named source, at a known time, inside a scope owned by a rauthy subject, carrying a trust class that decides whether a client may act on it and a status that decides whether it is still true. The type system enforces the parts that matter: there is no way to construct a memory without provenance, and no way to promote one to instruction grade without an authority token that only a human decision produces.\n",
    "title": "The memory record: scope, trust class, actor, provenance, and the states a memory can be in"
  },
